# ARTEX network detection - Suricata rules
# Repo:  https://github.com/jiwoochris/artex-global
# Guide: ../../docs/defense-en.md
# Index & how-to-test: detections/suricata/README.md
#
# SCOPE AND HONESTY - read before deploying:
#  - These rules target the one ARTEX artifact observable on the wire: the norma SDK's
#    WebFetch User-Agent "norma/0.4" (github.com/Autumn-27/norma/tool/webfetch.go).
#    The recording proxy (traffic/traffic.go) does not modify request headers, so that
#    UA reaches the target on the wire. Other worker tools (curl, nmap, etc.) use their
#    own default User-Agents — detect those with generic scanner signatures and ../sigma/.
#  - The enrichment prober carries NO distinctive User-Agent. It presents the
#    engagement's masked browser identity through the masking egress proxy, so it is
#    indistinguishable from ordinary browser traffic at the target and there is no
#    network indicator for it. Detect enrichment by behaviour (../sigma/correlation/
#    and the defense guide) instead.
#  - The norma User-Agent is only visible where traffic is plaintext HTTP or inspected
#    at a TLS-terminating proxy / WAF. End-to-end TLS encrypts it.
#  - A static User-Agent can be changed by the operator; its absence does NOT imply safety.
#  - The self-update User-Agent "artex-selfupdate" travels over HTTPS to GitHub and is not
#    network-observable (TLS SNI alone is too common to alert on) - intentionally omitted.
#  - The audit-control marker is an operator-side log artifact, not target-facing traffic -
#    detect it with ../sigma/artex_guard_audit_framing.yml instead.

alert http any any -> any any (msg:"ARTEX worker WebFetch User-Agent (norma)"; flow:established,to_server; http.user_agent; content:"norma/"; startswith; fast_pattern; classtype:attempted-recon; reference:url,github.com/jiwoochris/artex-global/tree/main/detections/suricata; metadata:created_at 2026_10_07; sid:1000003; rev:1;)
